<<<
+ like, adj. For & the l., see FORTH 1. like in questionable constructions.
===
**like**, adj. For *& the l.*, see ^^FORTH^^ 1.

**like** in questionable constructions.
>>>

<<<
1. It will be best to dispose first of what is, if it is a misuse at all, the most flagrant & easily recognizabie misuse of like.
===
**1.** It will be best to dispose first of what is, if it is a misuse at all, the most flagrant & easily recognizable misuse of *like*.
>>>

<<<
Unfortunately few have observed like you have done.
===
*Unfortunately few have observed like you have done.*
>>>

<<<
the substitution of as for like in their sentences would sound artificial.
===
the substitution of *as* for *like* in their sentences would sound artificial.
>>>

<<<
But in good writing this particular like is very rare,
===
But in good writing this particular *like* is very rare,
>>>

<<<
& to whom like was once the natural word, deliberately weed it out.
===
& to whom *like* was once the natural word, deliberately weed it out.
>>>

<<<
is:—Or can these tickets be kept (like the sugar ecards were) by the retailer?/The retail price can never reach a prohibitive figure like petrol has done./Wasub’s words sank into Lingard’s heart like lead sinks into water.
===
is:—*Or can these tickets be kept* (like the sugar cards were) *by the retailer?/The retail price can never reach a prohibitive figure* like *petrol has done./Wasub's words sank into Lingard's heart* like *lead sinks into water.*
>>>

<<<
/They studied the rules of a game like a lawyer would study an imperfectly-drawn-up will./Our great patron saint * St George’ was a Greek,
===
/*They studied the rules of a game* like *a lawyer would study an imperfectly-drawn-up will./Our great patron saint 'St George' was a Greek,*
>>>

<<<
+ like a good many of the saints are./ The idea that you can learn the technique of an art like you can learn the multiplication table or the use of logarithms.
===
+ *like a good many of the saints are./The idea that you can learn the technique of an art* like *you can learn the multiplication table or the use of logarithms.*
>>>

<<<
2. The rest of this article is intended for those who decide against the conjunctional use
===
**2.** The rest of this article is intended for those who decide against the conjunctional use
>>>

<<<
& in all of them like may be regarded as an adjective or adverb having the additional power (cf. worth) of | directly governing nouns as if it were a preposition.
===
& in all of them *like* may be regarded as an adjective or adverb having the additional power (cf. *worth*) of directly governing nouns as if it were a preposition.
>>>

<<<
Examples are:—Or should he have a palace some distance away, like the Bishop of Winchester has at Farnham?/But in an industrialized county like so great a part of Lancashire is, the architecture can hardly fail to. ../
===
Examples are:—*Or should he have a palace some distance away,* like the Bishop of Winchester has *at Farnham?/But in an industrialized county* like so great a part of Lancashire is, *the architecture can hardly fail to.../*
>>>

<<<
The club doctor was the friend & adviser of iis members, something like the country parson has to be to his labour parishioners in the present day.
===
*The club doctor was the friend & adviser of its members,* something like the country parson has to be *to his labour parishioners in the present day.*
>>>

